<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Use of Technology in School</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. Concept: Simplify complex concepts for group comprehension.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on technology use by encouraging classmates to share opinions (e.g., asks peers to share their favorite school app and explain why it's their favorite).
Can simplify complex technology terms by using simple words and examples (e.g., explains Wi-Fi as the internet that connects phones and computers without wires).
Can clarify technology terms using both English and familiar language to help peers (e.g., explains "download" in both languages if classmates are unsure).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a quick "Tech Term Challenge." The teacher displays 3-4 technology terms (e.g., "cloud storage," "algorithm," "digital footprint") on the board. Students work in pairs to try and explain these terms to each other using simple words. They should discuss what they *can* use technology for and what they *should* avoid, activating their knowledge of vocabulary related to *apps*, *devices*, and *benefits* from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a complex technology term (e.g., "synchronize") in simple language, providing a clear example: "To 'synchronize' means to make two or more things the same at the same time, like when your phone and computer *update* the same list of *apps*." The teacher also models how to ask a classmate to clarify an opinion using Wh-questions: "What *problem* *does* this app *solve*?" and how to rephrase for clarity, focusing on correct stress in words like "information" and "network" and intonation with "should" and "can."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I don't understand a word, what *should* I ask my friend to do?" (Expected answer: "Explain it simply" or "Give an example.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain a *solution* for a *problem*, *should* I use complicated words or easy words?" (Expected answer: "Easy words.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post excerpt (2-3 paragraphs, A2.4 level) reviewing an educational app (e.g., "Quizlet" or "Padlet"). The blog post briefly explains the app's *benefits*, some features, and suggests how students *can* *use* it. It also includes an example of a potentially complex term simplified within the text. The blog post will integrate target vocabulary like *platform, resources, useful, incorporate*, and *recommend*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the main idea and key details of a short, simple blog post reviewing a technology tool, specifically focusing on how information is simplified and presented for a general audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main *purpose* of this blog post?" (Answer: To review and explain an *educational app*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match a complex term from the text (e.g., "interactive platform") with its simpler explanation provided within the text or by the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The blog post says this app *should* only be used by teachers." (False) "The app *can* help students access *resources*." (True).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying technology concepts, facilitating discussions, and using "simple present" for opinions, "should/can" for suggestions/ability, and Wh-questions. Focus on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "education," "innovative") and intonation with modals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Simplification:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 sentences containing complex technology language related to the theme. Students work individually to rewrite them using simpler terms and examples. E.g., "The *integration* of *digital tools* facilitates a more *dynamic curriculum*." (Simplified: "Using computers helps make school lessons more active.") They *should* use vocabulary like *tools, curriculum, digital*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Explain a Tech Term:</b> In pairs, students receive a card with a tech term from the vocabulary list (e.g., "*platform*," "*software*," "*network*," "*innovation*," "*access*"). One student explains the term to their partner using simple words and an example, without saying the word. The partner guesses the term. They *can* use "What *does*... *mean*?" and "Can you give an example?" to ask for clarification, practicing stress and intonation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Facilitate a Mini-Discussion:</b> Students work in groups of three. One student acts as a "facilitator." They introduce a topic related to technology in school (e.g., "What *apps* *should* we *use* for presentations?" or "What *are* the *benefits* and *problems* of using *devices* in class?") and encourage the other two to share their opinions, ensuring everyone understands by asking "What *do* you *mean* by that?" or "Can you explain that *solution* simply?" The facilitator *should* use Wh-questions and rephrase when needed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Tech Review Blog Project Explanation & Discussion." Students work in their pre-assigned project groups for the "Tech Review Blog." Each group presents their chosen educational app for the blog. One student from each group acts as a "Project Explainer" and another as a "Discussion Facilitator." The Explainer presents their app, simplifying its features and *benefits* for the class, using target vocabulary (e.g., "This *platform* *is* *beneficial* *because* it *solves* the *problem* of..."). The Facilitator then opens the floor for a brief Q&A, asking classmates "What *are* your *opinions* on this app?" or "What *innovations* *do* you *think* this app *offers*?" They *should* ensure all questions are understood and answers are clear, rephrasing or asking for examples if necessary. They *should* also mention appropriate limits for using the app, for example, "You *shouldn't* *ignore* your classmates *while* using this app for group work."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in mediating information about their blog project, clarifying concepts, encouraging interaction, and utilizing simple present, "should/can" for suggestions and abilities, and Wh-questions. Emphasis on clear pronunciation of multisyllabic words and appropriate intonation for questions and suggestions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student (especially the designated Explainer and Facilitator) is formatively assessed on their ability to mediate the "Tech Review Blog" project. The teacher observes their explanations and facilitative skills during Stage 4. Students submit a brief written summary (3-4 sentences) of the app discussed in their group, ensuring they simplify one key feature and offer one *recommendation* using "should."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist/Observation):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Simplifies complex *technology* terms using simple words and examples.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitation skills: Encourages classmates to share *opinions* and asks clarifying questions (Wh-questions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar: Correctly uses simple present for opinions, "should" for suggestions, and "can" for abilities.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary: Incorporates at least 5-7 words from the recommended list (e.g., *app, benefit, problem, solution, device, use, recommend*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Understandable pronunciation, especially stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "educational," "innovative") and intonation with modals.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I *learned* that when I explain *technology*, I *should*..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One *problem* I *had* when explaining or facilitating was... and next time I *can* try to..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* mediating information, like explaining *apps* for our *Tech Review Blog*, help me in other school subjects or in daily life?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *strategies* did I *use* today to make sure my classmates understood my explanations or opinions about *technology*?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this mediation lesson? The group discussions and project explanations allowed students to genuinely practice mediating. Students actively used "should" and "can" for suggestions and abilities. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next unit? Perhaps provide more sentence frames for asking clarification questions for shy students. Focus more on stress for compound words like "software" and "network" in future lessons if needed. How will this connect to the next lesson? This lesson concludes the unit on "The Use of Technology in School." The next unit will build on collaborative skills and critical thinking, potentially through a different scenario, leveraging the communication and mediation skills developed here.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson completes the "Technology in Education" theme by having students mediate and present their "Tech Review Blog" project. The communication and clarification skills developed here, especially in simplifying complex ideas and facilitating discussions, will be crucial for any future collaborative projects or presentations in other themes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>